// THE `u64` ORDER FIX, ASKED AT EACH OF ITS OWN SEAMS — the three questions the
// four fixtures it closed do NOT ask, plus the controls that make each one a
// finding rather than a coincidence.
//
// `wql_domain_runtime_order_c`, `wql_value_domain_tiers_measured` and
// `wql_domain_incr_disagreement` each pin an END-TO-END answer of a query over a
// `u64` column. All three go green if the answer is right FOR ANY REASON. This
// file pins the three intermediate facts the remedy actually consists of, so
// that a later change breaking one of them says WHICH one:
//
//   1. THE PLUMB — the catalog carries "this column's order moved". A `u64`
//      column's declared type name reaches `SchemaCatalog` at all. Before the
//      fix nothing did: `classify_field_type` wrote the class REPRESENTATIVE
//      (`"ty":"i64"`) into the blob and the field's own name was gone before the
//      blob existed, so no comparison site downstream could have asked. Asserted
//      through `field_ord_wrap`, which reads the catalog and nothing else — the
//      plumb fails here even if every comparison site is perfect.
//   2. THE SEEDS — `min`/`max` over a group of ONE row answer that row's value,
//      for every value. This is the arm the accumulator SEEDS control and the
//      only one that can catch them: with more than one row a wrong seed is
//      usually displaced by some row and the answer comes out right anyway.
//   3. THE COMPARE — `min`/`max`/`order by` over a group of three rows that
//      straddle 2^63.
//
// ── WHY (2) IS ITS OWN QUESTION, AND THE ARITHMETIC THAT SAYS SO ────────────
// The remedy compares through `el_wrap_ord_key(x) = x ^ i64::MIN`, so the SEEDS
// must be that key's extrema, not the signed order's. They are not the same
// numbers and they are not close:
//     key(i64::MIN) = i64::MIN ^ i64::MIN = 0            ← an INTERIOR key
//     key(i64::MAX) = i64::MAX ^ i64::MIN = -1           ← an INTERIOR key
//     key(0)        = i64::MIN                           ← the LEAST key
//     key(-1)       = i64::MAX                           ← the GREATEST key
// So `max` must seed RAW 0 and `min` must seed RAW -1 (u64 0 and u64 2^64-1 —
// the identity elements of max and min over an unsigned column). Had the signed
// seeds been left in place under the new compare, `max` over the single row
// {5u64} would answer i64::MIN — u64 2^63, A VALUE PRESENT IN NO ROW — because
// key(5) = i64::MIN+5 is LESS than key(i64::MIN) = 0. That is the exact defect
// `6440e565` fixed for f64 (`min` over {+inf} answering 1.797e308), reappearing
// in another type family the moment its comparator moved. Block 2 is the arm
// that would catch it: it asserts 5, not 2^63.
//
// ⚠ EVERY NUMBER BELOW IS HAND-DERIVED FROM THE UNSIGNED ORDER AND THE
// TWO'S-COMPLEMENT READING, never read off a run, and nothing here asks `el_ty`,
// `ElTy.ord` or `el_wrap_ord_key` what the answer is — an oracle that shares its
// rule with the code under test agrees with itself by construction
// (`feedback_oracle_must_be_independent`). The three rows are
//     id 0: 5                     — u64 5,                     i64 5
//     id 1: 9223372036854775808   — u64 2^63,                  i64 -9223372036854775808
//     id 2: 18446744073709551615  — u64 2^64-1,                i64 -1
// Unsigned ascending is ids 0, 1, 2. SIGNED ascending is ids 1, 2, 0 — a
// DIFFERENT permutation of the same three rows, which is what makes the order
// blocks able to fail rather than merely to agree.
//
// ── THE CONTROLS, AND WHAT EACH ONE RULES OUT ───────────────────────────────
//   • an `i64` column in the SAME schema, over the SAME kind of extremes: if the
//     key were applied to the integer CLASS rather than to the derived
//     order-fidelity of each type, this column would move and it must not;
//   • a `u32` column, which is unsigned and whose order was NEVER wrong (every
//     value is non-negative in i64). `ElTy.ord` says WRAP only when the value
//     does not fit, so u32 must report `field_ord_wrap == false` — the fix keyed
//     on "is it unsigned" instead would flip this and change nothing observable,
//     which is precisely why the assertion is on the CATALOG BIT and not only on
//     an answer;
//   • `sum` over the u64 column, which is a DIFFERENT seam of the same type
//     loss and which the order fix did not answer. 5 + 2^63 + (2^64-1) =
//     27670116110564327428 EXCEEDS 2^64-1, so the unsigned answer is an
//     overflow error; the accumulator now adds under the column's own overflow
//     predicate and says so. This block was written when that was an open
//     decision and asserted -9223372036854775804 instead — the i64 sum of the
//     three payloads, reached with no i64 overflow — which pinned the defect as
//     correct. What it pins now is that the two seams stay independent.
//
// EXIT CODES: 10s = the plumb, 20s = the seeds (dynamic), 30s = the seeds
// (incremental), 40s = the compare over three rows (dynamic), 50s = the compare
// (incremental), 60s = `order by`, 70s = the controls.
//
// exit 0 = each seam of the u64 order fix holds independently.
package test;

use logos.lang.writ.container;
use logos.lang.writ.anyval;
use logos.lang.writ.array;
use logos.std.wql.ir;
use logos.std.wql.catalog_macro;
use logos.std.wql.wql;
use logos.mem.deem;
use logos.lang.result;
use logos.lang.str;
use logos.mem.collections.vec;

pub schema W : code(0x0E68_0000_0000_0001) {
    id: i64 = 0,
    g:  i64 = 1,
    x_u64:   u64   = 2,
    x_usize: usize = 3,
    x_u32:   u32   = 4,
    x_i64:   i64   = 5
}
resource cat = schema_catalog!{ W };

fn mkw(d: &Writ, id: i64, g: i64, xu: u64, xs: usize, x3: u32, xi: i64) -> WAny {
    let r: W = d.make::<W>();
    r.id = id; r.g = g; r.x_u64 = xu; r.x_usize = xs; r.x_u32 = x3; r.x_i64 = xi;
    return WAny::ref_to((r.m as i64) as *const u8);
}

fn main() -> i32 {
    let d: Writ = writ_new(65536i64);
    let c: SchemaCatalog = cat();

    // ══ 1. THE PLUMB — the catalog knows which columns' orders moved ══════════
    // No query, no engine: this is the fact reaching the runtime at all.
    if !c.field_ord_wrap("W", "x_u64")   { return 10i32; }
    if !c.field_ord_wrap("W", "x_usize") { return 11i32; }
    // and the columns whose order did NOT move must say so. u32 is the sharp one:
    // unsigned, but every value fits i64 non-negative, so the signed compare was
    // already this column's own order and keying it would be a change with no
    // defect behind it.
    if c.field_ord_wrap("W", "x_u32")    { return 12i32; }
    if c.field_ord_wrap("W", "x_i64")    { return 13i32; }
    if c.field_ord_wrap("W", "id")       { return 14i32; }
    // an unknown field is not a licence to guess
    if c.field_ord_wrap("W", "nope")     { return 15i32; }
    if c.field_ord_wrap("Nope", "x_u64") { return 16i32; }

    // three rows straddling 2^63, each its OWN group in `g` for the seed blocks
    let a: &mut WArray<WAny> = d.array(4i64);
    a.push(mkw(&d, 0i64, 100i64, 5u64,                    5usize,                    5u32,   5i64));
    a.push(mkw(&d, 1i64, 101i64, 9223372036854775808u64,  9223372036854775808usize,  70000u32, -9223372036854775808i64));
    a.push(mkw(&d, 2i64, 102i64, 18446744073709551615u64, 18446744073709551615usize, 4294967295u32, -1i64));
    // the SAME three rows again, all in ONE group, for the compare blocks
    let b: &mut WArray<WAny> = d.array(4i64);
    b.push(mkw(&d, 0i64, 7i64, 5u64,                    5usize,                    5u32,   5i64));
    b.push(mkw(&d, 1i64, 7i64, 9223372036854775808u64,  9223372036854775808usize,  70000u32, -9223372036854775808i64));
    b.push(mkw(&d, 2i64, 7i64, 18446744073709551615u64, 18446744073709551615usize, 4294967295u32, -1i64));

    let mut e: QEnv = QEnv::new();
    e.bind_source("ss", WAny::ref_to(a as *const WArray<WAny> as *const u8));
    e.bind_source("ts", WAny::ref_to(b as *const WArray<WAny> as *const u8));

    // ══ 2. THE SEEDS — ONE-ROW GROUPS, DYNAMIC ════════════════════════════════
    // For a group of one row, `min` and `max` are both that row's value. A seed
    // that is not an extremum of the compared order wins against the row and
    // answers a value present in no row — see the arithmetic in the header.
    let q1: Query = match Query::compile(
        "from ss s group by s.g aggregate lo = min(s.x_u64), hi = max(s.x_u64) select (key, lo, hi)", &c) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 20i32; } };
    let r1: QRows = match q1.run(&e) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 21i32; } };
    if r1.row_count() != 3i64 { return 22i32; }
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < 3i64 {
        let k: i64 = r1.get_i64(i, 0i64);
        // the row value this group holds, as i64 bits: 5 / i64::MIN / -1
        let mut want: i64 = 0i64;
        if k == 100i64      { want = 5i64; }
        else if k == 101i64 { want = -9223372036854775808i64; }
        else if k == 102i64 { want = -1i64; }
        else                { return 23i32; }
        if r1.get_i64(i, 1i64) != want { return 24i32; }   // min of a one-row group
        if r1.get_i64(i, 2i64) != want { return 25i32; }   // max of a one-row group
        i = i + 1i64;
    }

    // ══ 3. THE SEEDS — ONE-ROW GROUPS, INCREMENTAL ════════════════════════════
    // The incremental engine's min/max rescan is SEED-FREE by construction (the
    // first live map key IS the running best), so this block is not a second copy
    // of block 2: it asks whether the OTHER engine reaches the same answers by
    // its own route. A disagreement here would be an engine disagreement, which
    // is the class this whole family exists to catch.
    let q2: Query = match Query::compile(
        "from ss s group by s.g aggregate lo = min(s.x_u64), hi = max(s.x_u64) select (key, lo, hi)", &c) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 30i32; } };
    let g2: IncrJoin = match q2.incremental(&e) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 31i32; } };
    if g2.had_error() { return 32i32; }
    let s2: QRows = g2.snapshot();
    if s2.row_count() != 3i64 { return 33i32; }
    let mut j: i64 = 0i64;
    while j < 3i64 {
        let k2: i64 = s2.get_i64(j, 0i64);
        let mut want2: i64 = 0i64;
        if k2 == 100i64      { want2 = 5i64; }
        else if k2 == 101i64 { want2 = -9223372036854775808i64; }
        else if k2 == 102i64 { want2 = -1i64; }
        else                 { return 34i32; }
        if s2.get_i64(j, 1i64) != want2 { return 35i32; }
        if s2.get_i64(j, 2i64) != want2 { return 36i32; }
        j = j + 1i64;
    }

    // ══ 4. THE COMPARE — ONE GROUP OF THREE, DYNAMIC ══════════════════════════
    // min = 5 (u64 5), max = 2^64-1 (i64 bits -1). Under the SIGNED order the
    // same three rows give min = 2^63 (i64::MIN) and max = 5, so a regression
    // here cannot look like a rounding difference.
    // `usize` rides the same query to pin that it is not a separate rule.
    let q3: Query = match Query::compile(
        "from ts s group by s.g aggregate lo = min(s.x_u64), hi = max(s.x_u64), ls = min(s.x_usize), hs = max(s.x_usize) select (lo, hi, ls, hs)", &c) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 40i32; } };
    let r3: QRows = match q3.run(&e) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 41i32; } };
    if r3.row_count() != 1i64 { return 42i32; }
    if r3.get_i64(0i64, 0i64) != 5i64  { return 43i32; }
    if r3.get_i64(0i64, 1i64) != -1i64 { return 44i32; }
    if r3.get_i64(0i64, 2i64) != 5i64  { return 45i32; }
    if r3.get_i64(0i64, 3i64) != -1i64 { return 46i32; }

    // ══ 5. THE COMPARE — ONE GROUP OF THREE, INCREMENTAL ══════════════════════
    let q4: Query = match Query::compile(
        "from ts s group by s.g aggregate lo = min(s.x_u64), hi = max(s.x_u64) select (lo, hi)", &c) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 50i32; } };
    let g4: IncrJoin = match q4.incremental(&e) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 51i32; } };
    if g4.had_error() { return 52i32; }
    let s4: QRows = g4.snapshot();
    if s4.row_count() != 1i64 { return 53i32; }
    if s4.get_i64(0i64, 0i64) != 5i64  { return 54i32; }
    if s4.get_i64(0i64, 1i64) != -1i64 { return 55i32; }

    // ══ 6. `order by` OVER THE SAME THREE ROWS, DYNAMIC ═══════════════════════
    // unsigned ascending = ids 0, 1, 2; signed ascending = ids 1, 2, 0.
    let q5: Query = match Query::compile("from ts s select s.id order by s.x_u64", &c) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 60i32; } };
    let r5: QRows = match q5.run(&e) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 61i32; } };
    if r5.row_count() != 3i64 { return 62i32; }
    if r5.get_i64(0i64, 0i64) != 0i64 { return 63i32; }
    if r5.get_i64(1i64, 0i64) != 1i64 { return 64i32; }
    if r5.get_i64(2i64, 0i64) != 2i64 { return 65i32; }

    // ══ 7. THE CONTROLS ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    // (a) the i64 column over the same extremes — the SIGNED order is this
    //     column's own order and must be untouched: min = i64::MIN, max = 5,
    //     ascending ids 1, 2, 0. If the key were applied by CLASS these would
    //     become 5 / -1 / 0,1,2, i.e. exactly the u64 answers above.
    let q6: Query = match Query::compile(
        "from ts s group by s.g aggregate lo = min(s.x_i64), hi = max(s.x_i64) select (lo, hi)", &c) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 70i32; } };
    let r6: QRows = match q6.run(&e) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 71i32; } };
    if r6.row_count() != 1i64 { return 72i32; }
    if r6.get_i64(0i64, 0i64) != -9223372036854775808i64 { return 73i32; }
    if r6.get_i64(0i64, 1i64) != 5i64                    { return 74i32; }

    let q7: Query = match Query::compile("from ts s select s.id order by s.x_i64", &c) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 75i32; } };
    let r7: QRows = match q7.run(&e) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 76i32; } };
    if r7.row_count() != 3i64 { return 77i32; }
    if r7.get_i64(0i64, 0i64) != 1i64 { return 78i32; }
    if r7.get_i64(1i64, 0i64) != 2i64 { return 79i32; }
    if r7.get_i64(2i64, 0i64) != 0i64 { return 80i32; }

    // (b) the u32 column — unsigned, order never moved. Values 5 / 70000 /
    //     4294967295 ascend as ids 0, 1, 2 under BOTH orders, so this block
    //     cannot distinguish them and does not claim to; what it pins is that a
    //     column the catalog reports as non-wrapping still sorts, i.e. that the
    //     bit is consulted rather than assumed.
    let q8: Query = match Query::compile("from ts s select s.id order by s.x_u32", &c) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 81i32; } };
    let r8: QRows = match q8.run(&e) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 82i32; } };
    if r8.row_count() != 3i64 { return 83i32; }
    if r8.get_i64(0i64, 0i64) != 0i64 { return 84i32; }
    if r8.get_i64(1i64, 0i64) != 1i64 { return 85i32; }
    if r8.get_i64(2i64, 0i64) != 2i64 { return 86i32; }

    // (c) `sum` over the u64 column — a SEPARATE seam of the same type loss,
    //     and the open decision this file recorded has since been taken.
    //     5 + 2^63 + (2^64-1) = 27670116110564327428, which EXCEEDS 2^64-1, so
    //     the honest unsigned answer is an overflow error. What this file used
    //     to assert here was −9223372036854775804: not a wrap, but the i64 sum
    //     of the three payloads, arrived at with no i64 overflow at all — the
    //     accumulator asking the i64 overflow predicate about a u64 sum. That
    //     assertion pinned the defect as correct, which is what an "open
    //     decision" costs when it is written into a fixture as a number.
    //     The accumulator now adds under the column's own predicate
    //     (`el_wrap_add`); `wql_u64_sum_accumulator` is where that seam is
    //     asked in both of its failure directions. What is still pinned HERE is
    //     that the two seams are independent: the ORDER fix above did not
    //     answer the arithmetic question, and this block would go red if a
    //     later change made `sum` silent again.
    let q9: Query = match Query::compile(
        "from ts s group by s.g aggregate t = sum(s.x_u64) select t", &c) {
        Result::Ok(v) => { v } Result::Err(_e) => { return 87i32; } };
    match q9.run(&e) {
        Result::Ok(_v) => { return 88i32; }
        Result::Err(_e) => { }
    }

    return 0i32;
}
